A man has 53 socks in his drawer. They are either red, blue, or white. In complete darkness, what is the minimum number of socks he must take out to guarantee a matching pair?

Since there are only three possible colors (red, blue, and white), picking three socks could result in one of each color. According to the Pigeonhole Principle, the fourth sock must match one of the previous three colors, guaranteeing a pair.

